  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\n- Several rounds of showers and Storms tomorrow \n\nForecast remains fairly consistent. There will be two rounds of \nshowers tomorrow. The first will be early in the morning. It will\nmove across Lower Michigan from the northwest and move eastward with\nheavy rain possible. Severe winds not expected but there is the \npotential for strongest storms to overperform. \n\nAnother line in the afternoon and evening will trek across\nSouthern Michigan along the warm air advection. The best \ninstability will be in IN and OH. However, due to the significant\nPWATS, upwards of 2 inches, CAPE's upwards of 1500 J/kg, lapse \nrates of 7 C/kg through the mid to low levels along with strong \nlow level wind shear and there will be a chance for convection \nalong and south of the I 94 corridor could become strong to \nsevere. SPC continues to have a marginal risk through this region.\n\n\n- Chances for Rain through the week\n\n There remains some question about where the frontal pattern\nshifts this week. If it shifts/expands to the north then quite a \nbit of southern lower Michigan could receive multiple rounds of \nshowers and storms through Thursday. The zonal flow will keep mid \nlevel moisture over the area. If there is enough warm air \nadvection, along with any mid level short wave amplification, and \nareas could receive periods of heavy rain. NAEFS shows a \nprolonged period of anomalous moisture along the southern half of\nlower Michigan. Latest NAEFS PWATs along the I 96 corridor could\nbe around 1.25 inches and increase to 2 inches in northern IN. \nThis moisture boundary remains entrenched over the area through \nthe week and latest ensembles has it shifting northward towards \nthe end of the week. If it shifts southward, then much of the\nregion will remain dry. \n\nAnother system will lift into the area this weekend and could\nbring additional shower and thunderstorm chances Saturday.",
